@startuml
' Figure 7.4 – System sequence diagram: one assistant turn, answer or write
' Sprint 8 (FR-78, FR-80). Source: AiService.chat in src/ai/ai.service.ts.
!include ../_style.puml
mainframe interaction SequenceDiagram Assistant turn
autonumber "0 :"

participant "Writer" as R
participant "Assistant\npanel" as P
participant "System" as A
participant "Language\nmodel" as L
participant "Database" as D

ref over R, D : Sign in

R -> P : Type or dictate a brief
activate P
P -> A : POST /ai/chat with the messages,\nthe article and the selection
activate A
A -> A : Check and grant the daily AI allowance

A -> D : Read the draft
activate D
D --> A : Draft text
A --> P : Step "draft" done, with its word count
A -> D : Read the writer's style profile
D --> A : Memory block
A --> P : Step "profile" done
opt documents are attached to the article
  A -> D : Retrieve passages from the attached documents
  D --> A : Passages, with document and page
  A --> P : Step "documents" done, with counts
end
deactivate D

A --> P : Step "thinking" active
A -> L : Stream the routing call, with the write_to_article tool
activate L

alt the model answers in the panel
  L --> A : Text
  A --> P : Step "thinking" done, the answer streams as Markdown
  P --> R : Read the answer in the panel
else the model calls write_to_article(placement, brief)
  L --> A : Tool call
  A --> P : Step "thinking" done
  ref over P, D : In-document write (figure 7.5)
  A -> L : Continue the turn with the tool's result
  L --> A : One-line recap
  A --> P : The recap
end
deactivate L

A -> D : Record the interaction and settle the tokens of both calls
A --> P : Step "done"
deactivate A
P --> R : The steps collapse into one finished run
deactivate P

note over R, D
  The routing call may run at most three steps: decide, call the tool,
  recap. Each stage is reported only once it has actually run, so the
  panel shows the real pipeline, not an animation. A Stop still bills
  an estimate of what was produced.
end note
@enduml
